            "subject": "Add App Store in-app purchase to the iOS paywall",
            "body": "Add App Store in-app purchase to the iOS paywall\n\niOS 1.0 was rejected on Guideline 3.1.1: the app granted access to Premium\nbought on the web with no equivalent IAP path. Hiding checkout behind !isIOS\nwas not enough — the rule is about access, not UI visibility.\n\n- src/lib/purchases.ts owns every RevenueCat call. The SDK is imported lazily\n  so the Expo web bundle never loads a native module it cannot run, and every\n  function no-ops off iOS.\n- Identity is set from _setAuth/_clearAuth, the choke points every auth path\n  goes through, so app_user_id is the Vittio user id before a purchase can\n  start and does not leak to the next account on the device. Neither call is\n  awaited into sign-in: a RevenueCat outage degrades the paywall, not login.\n- The iOS paywall renders StoreKit-priced packages, purchase, and the Restore\n  Purchases button Apple requires. Prices come from the store, never from a\n  constant — a subscriber on a retired price is not paying today's number.\n- iOS never references the web in any state (Guideline 3.1.3 steering).\n  Apple-billed users are excluded from every Stripe surface, matching the\n  server, which now refuses them.\n\ne2e covers only what Expo web renders truthfully; the StoreKit flow is verified\nby hand against a Sandbox tester. login() gained user overrides — the app\nrefetches /user after sign-in and writes it back to the store, so overriding\nthe login response alone was silently reverted.\n\nAlso fixes a fixture bug: subscription_status was \"trialing\", which matches\nneither vocabulary the app uses, so every e2e user looked expired.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015XAnzXatq9LE84zyNc4yw9\n",
